  "controlNotes": [
    "The original Firefox control report has engine=firefox and Windows host. Its copied scope text incorrectly says Desktop WebKit. Treat that phrase as a template erratum: the experiment used Firefox, as its explicit engine and native response records show. The original report is preserved unchanged; the helper now formats engine dynamically.",
    "The original WebKit protocol control preceded the optional Firefox observer/engine additions to its helper. It independently reproduced native cached-media failures under protocol-offline with both readable and opaque AAC, without importing the Pletina Player; online-restored and origin-unavailable controls advanced. It recorded3 expected CORS diagnostics when a cors fetch tried reading an opaque copy, not product errors.",
    "The Firefox observer records an original-provider URL media alias from an opaque cached Response with status200 and fromServiceWorker=true, including when the real network is cut. Final offline queue assertions prohibit app API/fetch resolution and external fixture deliveries, while retaining those native SW observations.",
    "The QA TLS proxy previously represented route.abort as HTTP502, which no-cors fetch fulfills opaquely. Its final socket-abort implementation is independently checked with Playwright interception removed: actual socket failure rejects TypeError, while explicit HTTP502 remains fulfilled opaque.",
    "Historical WebKit v3 rows0/cacheEntries1 alone did not establish an orphan: the origin-gate host keeps navigator online and a resumed transfer may stage bytes while validating. The final explicit startup-GC scenario separately seeds one unreferenced valid stage and preserves a real committed pointer/body plus an unrelated key. Active-stage preservation is covered by product unit tests; no historical stage is retroactively declared completed.",
    "Full1662 and initial3c/v2/v3 raw results, failures, and exact QA sources remain separate. Their counts are not reassigned or added to final c2 selected-feature results."
  ],
